{
  "id": "u07",
  "number": 7,
  "title": "Everyday verbs (-er)",
  "summary": "Talk about what you speak, where you live and work, and what you like or hate. Most French verbs end in -er and follow one pattern. Ask questions with est-ce que, où, quand and pourquoi.",
  "canDo": [
    "Say what languages I speak, using parler for I, you, he or she, and they.",
    "Say where I live and what I do, using habiter and travailler.",
    "Say what I like, love and hate, with le, la or les in front of the thing.",
    "Ask simple questions with est-ce que, où, quand and pourquoi, and answer with parce que."
  ],
  "lessons": [
    {
      "id": "u07-l1",
      "title": "To speak",
      "goal": "Say which languages you and other people speak, using parler.",
      "notes": [
        {
          "id": "u07-l1-n1",
          "title": "Regular -er verbs",
          "body": "Most French verbs end in **-er**. Take off the **-er** to get the stem (*parl-*), then add the ending that matches who is doing the action: **je parle**, **tu parles**, **il / elle parle**, **ils / elles parlent**. The endings **-e**, **-es** and **-ent** all sound the same, so listen for the subject word instead.",
          "examples": [
            {
              "fr": "Je parle français.",
              "en": "I speak French."
            },
            {
              "fr": "Tu parles anglais ?",
              "en": "Do you speak English?"
            },
            {
              "fr": "Elles parlent anglais.",
              "en": "They speak English. (women)"
            }
          ],
          "more": "The **nous** and **vous** forms sound different: **nous** parl**ons** (we speak), **vous** parl**ez** (you speak, polite or plural). Learn the whole set as one pattern: parle, parles, parle, parlons, parlez, parlent."
        },
        {
          "id": "u07-l1-n2",
          "title": "Languages",
          "body": "Names of languages start with a small letter and usually have **le** or **l'** in front: *le français*, *l'anglais*. After **parler** you drop the article, so say *je parle français* (*je parle le français* is also correct, but less common). Use **aussi** (also) after the verb to add another language.",
          "examples": [
            {
              "fr": "Il parle anglais aussi.",
              "en": "He also speaks English."
            },
            {
              "fr": "Le français et l'anglais.",
              "en": "French and English."
            },
            {
              "fr": "Je parle français.",
              "en": "I speak French."
            }
          ]
        }
      ],
