    {
      "id": "u07-l4",
      "title": "Questions",
      "goal": "Ask simple questions and give a reason with parce que.",
      "notes": [
        {
          "id": "u07-l4-n1",
          "title": "Two easy ways to ask",
          "body": "Raise your voice at the end of a sentence to make it a question: *Tu parles anglais ?* You can also start with **est-ce que**, which has no meaning on its own. It turns any sentence into a question. Both are normal in speech, and there is always a space before the **?**.",
          "examples": [
            {
              "fr": "Tu habites ici ?",
              "en": "Do you live here?"
            },
            {
              "fr": "Est-ce que tu travailles ?",
              "en": "Are you working?"
            },
            {
              "fr": "Est-ce qu'il parle français ?",
              "en": "Does he speak French?"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "u07-l4-n2",
          "title": "Question words and because",
          "body": "**où** means *where*, **quand** means *when* and **pourquoi** means *why*. Put the question word first: *Où est Léa ?* To give a reason, start with **parce que** (*because*): *Parce que j'aime la musique.*",
          "examples": [
            {
              "fr": "Où est-ce que tu habites ?",
              "en": "Where do you live?"
            },
            {
              "fr": "Quand est-ce que tu travailles ?",
              "en": "When do you work?"
            },
            {
              "fr": "Parce que j'adore ça.",
              "en": "Because I love it."
            }
          ]
        }
      ],
      "words": [
        {
          "id": "u07-l4-w1",
          "fr": "est-ce que",
          "en": "question starter (no word needed in English)",
          "tip": "Put it in front of any sentence to make a question."
        },
        {
          "id": "u07-l4-w2",
